## Proof

**Setup.** Let $\Lambda$ be the ring of symmetric functions over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle$ be the standard (Hall) inner product, defined as the unique bilinear form on $\Lambda$ for which the Schur functions $\{s_\lambda\}$ form an orthonormal basis:
$$\langle s_\lambda, s_\mu \rangle = \delta_{\lambda,\mu}.$$

The **dual basis** of a basis $\{u_\lambda\}$ is the unique basis $\{u_\lambda^*\}$ satisfying
$$\langle u_\lambda, u_\mu^* \rangle = \delta_{\lambda,\mu}.$$

We claim: **the dual basis of the power-sum basis $\{p_\lambda\}$ is $\left\{\dfrac{p_\lambda}{z_\lambda}\right\}$**, where
$$z_\lambda := \prod_{i \ge 1} i^{m_i(\lambda)}\, m_i(\lambda)!\,,$$
and $m_i(\lambda)$ denotes the number of parts of $\lambda$ equal to $i$.

---

**Step 1: The power-sums are orthogonal.**

The key identity (Macdonald, *Symmetric Functions and Hall Polynomials*, Ch. I, §4) is:
$$p_\lambda = \sum_\mu \chi^\mu_\lambda \, s_\mu,$$
where $\chi^\mu_\lambda$ is the value of the irreducible character of $S_n$ indexed by $\mu$ on the conjugacy class of cycle-type $\lambda$. Since the Schur functions are orthonormal,
$$\langle p_\lambda, p_\rho \rangle = \sum_\mu \chi^\mu_\lambda \, \chi^\mu_\rho.$$

By the column orthogonality of irreducible characters,
$$\sum_\mu \chi^\mu_\lambda \, \chi^\mu_\rho = \begin{cases} z_\lambda & \text{if } \lambda = \rho, \\ 0 & \text{if } \lambda \neq \rho. \end{cases}$$

Therefore:
$$\boxed{\langle p_\lambda, p_\rho \rangle = z_\lambda\, \delta_{\lambda,\rho}.}$$

---

**Step 2: Extracting the dual.**

Since the $\{p_\lambda\}$ are mutually orthogonal (not merely biorthogonal with some other family), the dual basis is obtained by simple rescaling. Set
$$q_\lambda := \frac{p_\lambda}{z_\lambda}.$$

Then for any partitions $\lambda, \rho$ of the same size:
$$\langle p_\lambda,\, q_\rho \rangle = \left\langle p_\lambda,\, \frac{p_\rho}{z_\rho}\right\rangle = \frac{1}{z_\rho}\langle p_\lambda, p_\rho\rangle = \frac{z_\lambda\, \delta_{\lambda,\rho}}{z_\rho} = \delta_{\lambda,\rho},$$
using $z_\lambda = z_\rho$ whenever $\lambda = \rho$. For partitions of different sizes the inner product is $0$ since $\Lambda$ is graded and the inner product respects the grading.

The family $\{q_\lambda\} = \{p_\lambda / z_\lambda\}$ is a basis (each $q_\lambda$ is a nonzero scalar multiple of $p_\lambda$, and $\{p_\lambda\}$ is a basis), and it satisfies the duality relation by the computation above. By uniqueness of the dual basis, this is *the* dual basis.

---

**Step 3: Equivalent characterization via $\omega$.**

As a consistency check, recall the involutive algebra automorphism $\omega$ defined by $\omega(p_k) = (-1)^{k-1} p_k$. A standard identity states
$$\omega(p_\lambda) = \frac{p_\lambda}{z_\lambda} \cdot z_\lambda \cdot \prod_{i} (-1)^{(i-1)m_i} \cdot \cdots$$
More directly, one has the well-known identity
$$\omega(p_\lambda) = \varepsilon_\lambda\, p_\lambda, \qquad \varepsilon_\lambda = (-1)^{|\lambda| - \ell(\lambda)},$$
and the duality can equivalently be expressed through the relation $\langle f, g\rangle = \langle \omega(f), \omega(g)\rangle$ together with the expansion of $p_\lambda$ in the monomial and forgotten bases. The rescaling by $1/z_\lambda$ is the essential content.

---

**Conclusion.**

The dual basis of the power-sum symmetric functions $\{p_\lambda\}$ with respect to the standard (Hall) inner product is

$$\boxed{\left\{\,\frac{p_\lambda}{z_\lambda}\,\right\}_{\lambda}, \qquad z_\lambda = \prod_{i \ge 1} i^{\,m_i(\lambda)}\, m_i(\lambda)!\,.}$$

This follows from the orthogonality relation $\langle p_\lambda, p_\rho\rangle = z_\lambda\,\delta_{\lambda,\rho}$ (a consequence of character orthogonality for the symmetric group), which makes the power-sums an orthogonal — but not orthonormal — basis, so that the dual is obtained by the scalar rescaling $p_\lambda \mapsto p_\lambda / z_\lambda$.

